ion  intern refers to  any individual in the process of  satisfying a registration board's  experience requirements  this includes anyone not registered to  practice architecture in a u.s or  canadian jurisdiction graduates from  naab accredited programs architecture  students who acquire experience prior to  graduation and other qualified  individuals identified by a registration  board and this is all because there's a  huge difference between what you learned  in school and the real practice of  architecture  and unless you go through the program  and meet all the requirements you don't  have the right to call yourself an  architect and you don't have the right  to practice i don't know if you guys  know bob borson he writes the blog life  of an architect  he had a pretty interesting piece on  this and he puts it really well he says  not having the right to license really  boils down to  not having the right to be held  accountable for the public  for protecting their health safety and  welfare or in other words the right to  be sued for screwing up like i said i  really used to hate the word intern but  actually after going through the process  myself i  realized that  because there are so many things that we  do that  have to do with the well-being of the  public  and that if you can't be held  responsible for it then you should not  be allowed to call yourself an architect  i'm sorry interns i still support you in  the u.s  the aia supports the use of the term  architectural associate and design  professional instead of intern  but uh legally well it's it really  depends on your jurisdiction and your  state  but uh yeah legally in the eyes of naab  and cacb if you're not licensed you're  still referred to as an intern architect  so i am of two minds on this and  obviously i decided to get license but i  think both arguments are valid so i'm  going to present the case for both based  on my personal experience  and  my personal opinions  so these are the most common reasons  people say getting license is not worth  it people say it costs too much  it's expensive the cost of exams yearly  fees which for me is around a thousand  dollars a year  the fee to join the continuing education  and of course there's a liability and  responsibility and of course the good  old i've been working in the field for  10 years i already know how to be an  architect argument and the biggest  reason for me  um  would be like the liability and  responsibility aspect of it i have  always had like  side hustles when i was an intern  like i did small renovations or like  interior design jobs or drafting jobs on  the side for  friends or small clients  but as a licensed architect technically  you can't do any of that work so you can  only do work that you're kind of signing  off on as a professional  and  you're giving assurance that the  building's going to be built to a  certain standard in terms of like life  safety and accessibility  giving assurance that you've kind of  checked all the necessary building codes  to make sure of time what this means is  that let's say  i want to do a small renault for a  friend right and my friend wants me to  help him like redesign his apartment and  submit permits for  moving around a couple walls  ripping out the ceiling and he's gonna  do the construction himself or get a  couple of his friends to help like i  used to do this all the time draw it up  submit it with my name and with my  contact info  and we get a electrical or like a  mechanical engineer to  submit his letter of assurance and this  is completely legal you can do this as  an unlicensed designer now that i am a  licensed architect i need to take  responsibility to oversee the  construction  now  because my friend is not a reputable  contractor  i don't really want any part in that  and because if anything if anything goes  wrong it could get messy if these small  jobs are a big part of your income then  maybe it's not the best idea for you  another scenario i've seen is i've  actually seen this happen to quite a few  of my friends because there is quite a  big film industry here in vancouver  um so sometimes architects will work as  set designers or art directors  for the film industry  like designing movie sets  but  according to our local jurisdiction the  aibc  this is frowned upon because  once again  you're building things that's gonna last  a couple weeks or a couple months  and you're tearing it down right after  that and that's  that that's in conflict with the  responsibility to oversee construction  and to make good buildings  yeah  i know it's extremely  stringent  every jurisdiction is different so you  should check with yours  but i think yeah this is the main thing  to keep in mind if you are someone who  kind of wants to straddle different  industries and roles that involve  construction  but is not exactly the role of a  licensed architect before we go into my  next argument i want to share a little  message from our sponsor skillshare  now i'm gonna build the case for why you  should get licensed  based on my personal experience other  than the fact that your parents finally  believe that you're not delivering  coffee in the office  you also get knowledge and competence  respect and confidence  and money  so whether you get licensed or not the  internship process and the whole  examination process  is designed to make sure  that you are well equipped to design and  construct safe and  healthy buildings  it's designed to  make sure you have the ability to manage  projects and coordinate with your  consultants and it ensures that you  also understand all the legal  responsibilities of an architect and i  think there is a real difference between  learning through experience and actually  sitting down and studying for it  although learning through experience is  equally important it's very different  when i started working i felt very  overwhelmed with all of the amount of  things that i didn